--TEST--
The sealed door is the only door into a session too: `get` answers `null` for a name `setSecret` wrote, a plain `set` under that name is a separate value, and `remove` takes the plain one and leaves the sealed one — `rule:http-server/a-session-holds-a-secret-only-sealed`, `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary`
--SKIPIF--
<?nvs
// As the round-trip case beside this one: the record crosses the store
// `[cache.shared]` names, and a tree without one skips. The store is asked
// directly rather than through `Core\Session::start`, which refuses where no
// request arrived (`rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`) — and
// this scaffolding answers none whatever the case does, since a `--GET--`
// section describes the program under `--FILE--` alone.
try {
    Core\Cache::shared()->get("whether-a-session-case-has-a-store-to-cross");
} catch (Throwable $nowhere) {
    echo "skip - ", $nowhere->message, "\n";
}
--GET--
under=a-request
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `set` refuses a `secret` at the boundary and `setSecret` is where one goes
// instead. The other half of that is asserted here: nothing `setSecret` wrote
// is reachable through the members that read and write the record the ordinary
// way, so a program cannot arrive at the ciphertext by asking for the name.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];
secret string $token = "sk-live-7";

Core\Session::start();
Core\Session::setSecret("refresh", $token, $ring);

echo "get is blind to a sealed value: ",
    Core\Session::get("refresh") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// The two live in spaces of their own, so writing the ordinary way under the
// same name neither replaces the sealed value nor is replaced by it.
Core\Session::set("refresh", "plain");
echo "a plain set under the same name: ", Core\Session::get("refresh") as string, "\n";
echo "and the sealed value is untouched: ",
    (Core\Session::getSecret("refresh", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The same claim through the member that takes a key away: `remove` takes what
// `set` wrote and leaves what `setSecret` wrote.
Core\Session::remove("refresh");
echo "remove takes the plain value: ",
    Core\Session::get("refresh") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
echo "and leaves the sealed one: ",
    (Core\Session::getSecret("refresh", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// And the one thing here that is not a miss: a ring that cannot key anything
// is the program's own bug, so it is the exception class a program's bugs get
// rather than a `null` a caller would read as "nothing was stored".
array<secret bytes> $empty = [];
try {
    Core\Session::getSecret("refresh", $empty);
    echo "an empty ring read something", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $noKeys) {
    echo "an empty ring is a program bug and not a miss", "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# As the round-trip case beside this one: one shared store, and the grant over
# it, granted to the tree for the reason that case gives. `redis` is
# `tests/db/compose.yaml`'s service.
[capabilities.cache]
shared = true

[session]
backend = "shared"

[cache.shared]
url = "redis://127.0.0.1:16379"
--EXPECT--
get is blind to a sealed value: null
a plain set under the same name: plain
and the sealed value is untouched: yes
remove takes the plain value: null
and leaves the sealed one: yes
an empty ring is a program bug and not a miss
